Embedded React: scroll over a layer by copy; capture the fade cache on every worker, without thrashing.

Modified from Embedded React (Apache-2.0, Copyright 2026 Cory Lamming) at the
revision pinned in dependencies.lock.json, after the patches listed before this
one there. Changed files: engine/scene/compositor.c,
engine/tests/rendering/test_layers.c and engine/tests/rendering/test_parallel.c.
Not yet proposed upstream.

A ScrollView over an image the host draws as a layer (a page scrolling over its
backdrop) repainted its whole viewport on every frame of a scroll: the layer's
image counted as a foreign node painted into the viewport, and a full-screen one
covers more than half of it, so the copy gave up. A layer leaves only cleared,
transparent pixels in the framebuffer, the same at every pixel, so when it
covers the whole viewport and paints before the ScrollView it is now treated as
part of the uniform underlay: the rows move and only the exposed strip is
repainted. test_layers scrolls a list of opaque, translucent and empty rows over
a layer and compares it with a full repaint, pixel for pixel.

A fade-cache capture could not run inside a parallel slice, so the frame after a
fade began rendered single-core to capture the group. A full-screen group is
now captured by the slices themselves when the forked rect holds it whole and no
other group can be reading the cache that commit: each slice renders its own
rows of the group into the cache, and the cache takes the group after the join
only if every slice holding rows of it captured them; otherwise it is left
empty. One group per fork captures: the first slice to try claims it under the
host's serial lock, since two groups would write the same rows of the one
buffer. With the lock, a group the slices cannot capture in a frame no longer
asks for a single-core frame: a group whose content changed between frames (a
page whose blocks stagger in as it fades) alternated a parallel frame and a
single-core capture that never hit. Hosts without the lock keep the
single-core request. test_parallel
checks, in a build with workers and a fade cache, that every frame of a fade
with a second group dimming over it forks and matches the same frame rendered
single-core.

A group as large as the one fading in the slot no longer evicts it: two
full-screen fades at once (a page's ground and the page leaving Search) took
the slot from each other every frame, and each capture asked for a single-core
frame, so every other frame rendered single-core (33-48 ms of raster at
2560 x 1440 on an M5). The second group takes the strip path, as a smaller one
did already.
